150 to 117 and raising mean OVERALL F1 by +0.024 (gen_03/cand_0, 0.735 to 0.759).\n- Stacking action-pruning rules sequentially across generations (sets in gen 1 -> blocks/kills in gen 2 -> digs in gen 3) compounds gains cleanly, reducing invented events by more than half (from >230 to 117) and raising mean OVERALL F1 by +0.138 total (0.621 to 0.759).\n- Clarifying that deflected point-winning spikes are still rally-ending 'kills' and excluding soft free-ball passes from 'attacks' reached the highest single-run F1 of 0.777 (103 invented) and a 2-run mean of 0.766 (+0.007 over gen_03/cand_0), though it was rejected on confirmation (gen_04/cand_3).\n- Providing team jersey colors and valid roster numbers trades action/time precision for shirt number accuracy: in every generation it boosted number F1 (+0.014 in gen_02/cand_3, +0.043 in gen_03/cand_3, +0.019 in gen_04/cand_0) but degraded time/action F1 and increased invented events (e.g., 117 to 133 in gen_04/cand_0, dropping OVERALL F1 from 0.766 to 0.753).\n- Attempting to fix early serve timestamps via prompt instructions consistently fails to improve OVERALL F1 across all four generations, whether anchored to physical ball-hand contact (gen_01/cand_3: 0.653; gen_02/cand_2: 0.688), ~1s before receive (gen_03/cand_2: 0.736), or crossing the net (gen_04/cand_1: 0.752 vs 0.759 baseline).\n- Restricting 'attack' and 'kill' strictly to \"jumping overhead hits\" trades fewer invented events for worse recall, cutting inventions from 150 to 132 but increasing missed events from 39 to 46 and leaving OVERALL F1 flat at 0.735 (gen_03/cand_1).\n- Adding multi-contact sequence logic for digs (forbidding consecutive digs or digs by the attacking team) does not improve over simpler dig rules, slightly increasing invented events (117 to 121) and missed events (33 to 37) for an OVERALL F1 of 0.761 (gen_04/cand_2).\n- Ordering of rules matters for evaluation efficiency: applying dig restrictions in gen 3 after block/kill cleanup yielded a kept +0.024 mean gain (gen_03/cand_0), whereas applying it in gen 2 before block/kill cleanup yielded a smaller single-run score of 0.716 and was rejected (gen_02/cand_1).\n- Open question: Since in-prompt roster constraints repeatedly degraded action and time detection (gen_01/cand_2, gen_02/cand_3, gen_03/cand_3, gen_04/cand_0), can jersey numbers be constrained or mapped in a deterministic post-processing step to capture the +0.043 number F1 gain without hurting event detection?\n- Open question: Can the two components of gen_04/cand_3 (counting deflected winning spikes as rally-ending kills vs. excluding free balls from attacks) be tested separately to isolate which rule drove the drop to 103 invented events and the 0.777 peak score?\n- Open question: Since prompt instructions failed to fix the systematic 1.1s\u20131.7s early bias on serves across four attempts, would a deterministic post-processing timestamp shift (+1.0s on predicted serves) recover those ~12\u201316 missed serves without disrupting model inference?\n"}, "parent_tiers": {"overall": 0.7661, "time": 0.8491, "action": 0.8149, "team": 0.8109, "number": 0.5895}, "parent_counts": {"ok": 403, "wrong_team": 2, "wrong_action": 17, "missed": 33, "invented": 117}, "parent_per_type": {"serve": {"real": 97, "predicted": 107, "matched": 81, "f1": 0.794}, "receive": {"real": 86, "predicted": 86, "matched": 82, "f1": 0.953}, "dig": {"real": 70, "predicted": 102, "matched": 65, "f1": 0.756}, "set": {"real": 41, "predicted": 53, "matched": 36, "f1": 0.766}, "attack": {"real": 110, "predicted": 136, "matched": 100, "f1": 0.813}, "kill": {"real": 44, "predicted": 48, "matched": 40, "f1": 0.87}, "block": {"real": 7, "predicted": 7, "matched": 1, "f1": 0.143}}, "input": "evo2data/input_05.txt"}